# WebFleet

**One cockpit for all your websites.**

WebFleet is a KISS (Keep It Simple) website portfolio monitor for individuals, small web builders and micro-agencies.

Instead of logging into multiple platforms just to find out what needs attention, WebFleet gives you one simple dashboard for your entire website fleet.

## Demo

The included demo is a **static, zero-install prototype**. It uses sample websites so it can run directly from GitHub Pages.

It demonstrates:

- Website availability/status
- SSL status
- Domain status
- Update status
- Broken-link status
- Last check
- Portfolio summary
- Filtering by problem type

> **Important:** The fleet dashboard above still uses simulated demo data — it does not scan or modify those sample sites. The separate "Check a real URL" tool further down **does** make a real outbound DNS query to whatever domain you enter (via a public DNS-over-HTTPS resolver), and attempts a basic connection. Nothing is modified on the target site either way. See the in-page caveat directly below that tool in `index.html` for exactly what it can and can't honestly tell you.

## KISS Install — 5 minutes

### Option A — easiest: GitHub website

1. Create a new **public** GitHub repository named `webfleet`.
2. Add the files from this repository.
3. Make sure `index.html` is in the repository root.
4. Go to **Settings → Pages**.
5. Under **Build and deployment**, choose **Deploy from a branch**.
6. Select the `main` branch and `/ (root)`.
7. Save.
8. GitHub will publish the demo at your Pages address.

GitHub's documentation confirms that Pages can publish static files from a repository, including an `index.html` entry file.

### Option B — GitHub CLI

If you already have GitHub CLI installed:

```bash
gh repo create webfleet --public --source=. --push
```

Then enable GitHub Pages in the repository's Settings → Pages.

## Run locally

No server is required.

Simply open:

```text
index.html
```

in a browser.

Or, if you have Python installed:

```bash
python -m http.server 8000
```

Then open:

```text
http://localhost:8000
```

## Roadmap

### V1 — Portfolio visibility
- [x] Single dashboard
- [x] Demo fleet
- [x] Status indicators
- [x] Filters
- [x] KISS installation
- [x] Header stats computed live from the fleet data (V1 shipped with these
      hardcoded and silently wrong — fixed)
- [ ] Add your own sites to the demo

### V2 — Enter a URL, actually check it (real, with an honest ceiling)
- [x] Real DNS resolution check, via a public DNS-over-HTTPS resolver
- [x] Basic reachability attempt
- [ ] Accurate HTTP status, SSL certificate details, broken-link scanning —
      **not possible from a static page alone.** Browsers block a page from
      reading the real response (status code, headers, body) of a request
      to a different domain unless that domain explicitly grants permission
      (CORS) — that's a security restriction, not something skipped here.
      See the "Check a real URL" section's in-page caveat in `index.html` for the full explanation.

### V3 — Real scanning engine (shipped, via a scheduled GitHub Action)
GitHub Pages only serves static files — it cannot run server-side code. So
the checks V2 couldn't do client-side (accurate status, SSL, broken links)
run instead in a scheduled GitHub Action, which faces none of a browser's
CORS restrictions. It writes `results.json`; the page fetches it same-origin
(no CORS issue at all, since it's served from the same site) and renders it
in a new "Live Scan Results" section, separate from the old V1 simulated
demo — real and simulated data are never mixed into the same numbers.

- [x] Real HTTP status + response time (`requests`, real status codes —
      not V2's opaque approximation)
- [x] Real SSL certificate check — actual TLS handshake, real expiry date
      and issuer, via Python's `ssl` module (not possible from a browser
      at all for a cross-origin request — this is exactly the gap V2
      couldn't close)
- [x] Broken-link check — **scoped honestly**: checks links found on the
      homepage only, not a full site crawl. A real, bounded check, not a
      stand-in for a bigger one.
- [x] Scheduled scans, every 6 hours, via `cron` in the workflow (plus a
      manual trigger button in the repo's Actions tab)
- [ ] Email alerts — not built yet. The honest zero-config path is GitHub's
      own notifications (watch the repo; a failed Action run notifies you
      the way any Action failure does). Real email needs SMTP or a service
      like SendGrid, which means adding your own credentials as GitHub
      Secrets — a reasonable next addition, not included by default so V3
      works with zero configuration beyond editing `sites.json`.

#### Setting V3 up

1. Edit `sites.json` — a plain JSON array of the domains you want scanned
   (no `https://`, just the bare domain).
2. Push to GitHub. Go to **Settings → Actions → General → Workflow
   permissions**, and make sure **"Read and write permissions"** is
   selected — the scan workflow commits `results.json` back to the repo,
   which needs write access that isn't always on by default.
3. Go to the **Actions** tab, select **"WebFleet Scan"**, and click **"Run
   workflow"** to trigger the first scan manually rather than waiting up to
   6 hours.
4. Refresh the page — "Live Scan Results" should now show real data.

**If you're testing `scan.py` locally** and the SSL issuer looks wrong (a
company name that isn't the real certificate authority), you're very likely
behind a network or corporate proxy that intercepts and re-signs outbound
HTTPS traffic — the check itself is a standard TLS handshake and will read
the genuine certificate once running somewhere without that interception
(GitHub Actions' own runners don't intercept traffic this way).

### V4 — "What needs my attention?" (shipped)
A single prioritized list, now the first thing the dashboard shows — above
the raw per-site grid, not replacing it. Two decisions this makes on your
behalf, both real:

- [x] **Ranked by actual severity, not column order.** A broken TLS
      certificate (breaks HTTPS for every visitor) ranks above an
      ambiguous HTTP 403 (may not affect visitors at all), which ranks
      above a broken link on an otherwise-healthy page.
- [x] **Confidence is stated honestly, not flattened.** Issues are tagged
      either `CONFIRMED` (a real visitor would hit the same problem — TLS
      failures, 5xx errors, 404s, verified broken links) or `CHECK
      MANUALLY` (a 401/403/406/429 — the classic signature of a WAF
      blocking the scanner specifically, which may mean nothing is
      actually wrong for real visitors). These are never shown as the
      same severity of problem.
- [x] A related root cause is shown once, not twice — a TLS failure that
      also breaks the HTTP check surfaces as one card explaining both,
      not two cards implying two unrelated problems.
- [x] A genuinely healthy fleet shows a clear "nothing needs attention"
      state, not an empty-looking page.

### V5 — WordPress version detection (shipped, no credentials)
Detects whether a site runs WordPress via its own public generator tag
(`<meta name="generator" content="WordPress X.Y.Z">`), and compares it
against WordPress.org's own published latest version — the same public
API WordPress core itself uses to check for updates.

- [x] Detected purely from what a site already exposes publicly — no
      login, no API key, no credentials stored anywhere
- [x] Real version comparison (not string comparison — `6.9` vs `6.10`
      is the classic trap a naive comparison gets backwards)
- [x] Degrades honestly: if the generator tag was removed (common with
      security plugins), reports "not detected" rather than guessing; if
      WordPress.org's API is slow or unreachable, reports "couldn't
      determine latest version" rather than failing the whole scan
- [x] Distinguishes "checked and it's genuinely not WordPress" from
      "never got to check at all" — confirmed as a real, not theoretical,
      gap: IntentBound.com is WordPress, but the scanner's 403 response
      (see the HTTP section above) meant the homepage HTML was never
      fetched, so no generator-tag search was possible. The first version
      of this check reported "not detected" identically in both cases;
      it now reports "couldn't check — the homepage request itself
      failed" for the second, honestly different, situation.
- [x] Surfaces in the V4 attention panel as a `CONFIRMED` finding — this
      is a verified fact from the site's own markup, not a scanner
      artifact like an ambiguous 403

**What this deliberately does not do**, and why that's a separate
decision rather than a missing feature: it cannot see plugins, themes,
whether backups exist or are current, or install anything. All of that
needs authenticated access to each site (an Application Password or API
key per domain) — a real architectural change from everything above,
since V1 through this point never store a single credential. That's a
legitimate direction, just not one to slide into as "just the next
version number." A future addition, if wanted, would need its own
explicit decision about credential storage (GitHub Secrets, most likely)
before writing any code.

### V6 — Authenticated plugin update detection (shipped, 3 sites configured)
The credentialed step V5 deliberately deferred. Uses WordPress's own
**Application Passwords** (core feature since 5.6 — scoped, revocable,
no admin password ever exposed) to read installed plugin versions via
each site's own REST API, then cross-references each against
WordPress.org's public plugin directory for a real latest-version
comparison — same pattern already proven for WordPress core itself.

- [x] Read-only. Never installs, activates, or modifies anything on any
      site — this is a GET request, nothing more.
- [x] Per-site opt-in: `sites.json` entries can now be either a plain
      domain string (unchanged, no authenticated checks) or an object
      with a `wp_secret_key` (enables authenticated checks for that
      site). Only sites that explicitly opt in are touched this way.
- [x] Credentials never touch the repo. Read from GitHub Secrets at
      Action runtime as environment variables, named by the site's own
      short key (e.g. `CMG` for the username, `WP_APP_PASSWORD_CMG` for
      the password) — verified directly during development that no
      code path prints, logs, or otherwise exposes the actual values.
- [x] Honest per-plugin outcome, not a blanket pass/fail: a plugin
      that's actually listed on WordPress.org's public directory gets a
      real "outdated: true/false." A premium, custom, or privately
      distributed plugin — not on that directory — correctly reports
      "can't determine" rather than guessing either way.
- [x] Auth failures (e.g. a rotated or revoked Application Password)
      surface honestly in the V4 attention panel as their own finding,
      tagged `CHECK MANUALLY` — this reflects a problem with the
      monitoring setup itself, not necessarily the site.
- [ ] **Themes are not covered yet** — only plugins. An earlier version
      of this plan mentioned both; only the plugin half got built in
      this pass. A real, stated gap, not an oversight to gloss over.
- [ ] Backups, and actually installing updates, remain deliberately
      unbuilt — those are write operations against production sites,
      unattended, on a schedule. A different, larger conversation.

### Alerting — a single GitHub Issue, no new account or secret
The one real gap the versions above didn't close: the dashboard updates
every 6 hours, but nothing tells you when it does. Rather than add
UptimeRobot or a Discord webhook — a second account, a second dashboard,
a second thing that can disagree with WebFleet — this uses GitHub's own
Issues API with the Action's built-in `GITHUB_TOKEN`. Zero new secrets,
zero new services.

- [x] **Deliberately narrow**: only SSL failures, total connection
      failure, 5xx errors, and 404s create an alert. A 401/403/406/429
      (the classic WAF-blocking-the-scanner signature) never does —
      alerting on those would recreate the exact false-alarm noise the
      whole confidence-tagging system exists to prevent. Confirmed by
      testing against the real reported findings: Grokipaedia's
      certificate mismatch alerts, IntentBound and Weather.KE's 403s
      correctly stay silent.
- [x] One tracking issue, not a flood: a persistent problem updates the
      same issue rather than creating a new one every 6 hours; a
      resolved problem closes it automatically with a timestamp, rather
      than leaving a stale open issue behind.
- [x] Coded defensively around a real, reported GitHub API quirk: a
      GitHub community thread found that filtering issues by label via
      the query parameter doesn't always return only matching issues —
      results here are re-checked client-side (label AND title prefix)
      before being treated as the tracking issue, confirmed by testing
      the exact scenario where an unrelated, mislabeled issue is present.
- [x] The same SSL/HTTP root-cause deduplication already built into the
      dashboard is mirrored here — one alert for one underlying problem,
      not two for the same certificate failure.

**Setup**: none beyond what V6 already needed. `GITHUB_TOKEN` is
automatic in every Action run; this just needs `issues: write` added to
the workflow's permissions (already done). Notifications arrive however
you've configured GitHub's own notification settings for this repo (the
"Watch" button, email, or the GitHub mobile app) — no new account to
create.

### V7 — Multi-platform
Potential integrations for other website platforms and hosting providers.

## Philosophy

WebFleet is deliberately **not** another giant webmaster control panel.

The question it should answer first is:

> **"What needs my attention?"**

Everything else comes later.

